course: dictionary
lesson: 8855
title: Shoo: making things go away
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Shoo» is a small word with two main jobs. On its own, it is an interjection: a sound you make to send animals or people away. As a verb, you shoo someone by waving your arms and saying «shoo». The context tells you which job the word is doing.
explain: «The cat was on the table, so Mum shooed it off the kitchen counter.» Here shooed is a verb, and the clue is the cat on the table: Mum wants it to leave. Ask yourself: is someone making something go away? If yes, shoo is probably the right meaning.
explain: Look at the words next to shoo. «Shoo away» and «shoo off» both mean make something leave. «A shoo-in» is different: it is an informal phrase for someone sure to win, so the sentence will talk about winning, not about animals.
fact: Shoo is an interjection when it stands alone: «Shoo!»
fact: As a verb, shoo changes form: shoo, shoos, shooed, shooing.
fact: «Shooed» is the past tense: «Dad shooed the birds off the picnic.»
fact: «Shoo away» and «shoo off» mean make someone or something leave.
fact: A shoo-in is an informal phrase for someone who is sure to win.
exercise: «The hens were pecking at the vegetables, so Gran began to shoo them.» What does shoo mean here? | To feed the hens | To count the hens | *To make the hens leave | To paint the hens
exercise: «Ella is a shoo-in for the spelling prize.» What does shoo-in mean? | Someone who is lost | A type of shoe | Someone very quiet | *Someone who is sure to win
exercise: «The cat was shooed off the sofa.» Find and copy the past tense of shoo. | cat | *shooed | was | sofa
test: Which word is said to make animals or people go away? | *Shoo | Please | Thanks | Hello
test: What is the past tense of shoo? | *shooed | shooing | shoo | shoos
exam: Which sentence uses shoo to mean someone sure to win? | «Gran shoos the children outside.» | «The cat shoos quickly.» | *«Our team is a shoo-in for the cup.» | «The birds were shooed off the path.»
